Don't raise the pool size — this is a connection leak. The waiting queue is climbing (7 → 28 requests), not hitting a steady ceiling. A bigger pool only delays the next failure.

The problem is in `db.js:11-15`. If `fn(client)` throws an error, `client.release()` never runs and the connection leaks. Use try/finally:

```javascript
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This ensures the connection is released even if the callback throws.

