Add logging at two central points where requests already flow:

**1. HTTP layer in app.js:** Insert a middleware before the routes to log incoming requests and responses. Either use `morgan` (a standard Express logging library) or write a simple custom middleware that logs method, path, user ID from `req.user`, status code, and duration. This catches every request.

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on('finish', () => {
    console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} ${res.statusCode} ${Date.now() - start}ms user=${req.user?.id}`);
  });
  next();
});
```

This goes after `sessionUser` middleware so `req.user` is already set.

**2. Database layer in db.js:** Log each query and write with the SQL, parameters, duration, and whether it succeeded. Since all data operations flow through `query()` and `write()`, you get complete visibility without touching the routes:

```javascript
async function query(text, params) {
  const start = Date.now();
  try {
    const res = await pool.query(text, params);
    console.log(`query ${Date.now() - start}ms rows=${res.rows.length}`);
    return res.rows;
  } catch (err) {
    console.log(`query failed ${Date.now() - start}ms ${err.message}`);
    throw err;
  }
}
```

This approach requires no changes to the routes themselves. The centralization in `db.js` and middleware setup in `app.js` means logging is added once and covers everything.

Add a logging middleware to app.js after `sessionUser` runs. This way you have `req.user` available to log the authenticated user, and all routes flow through it.

The middleware would:
1. Record the request start time
2. Wrap or listen to the response ending (via `res.on('finish')` or patching `res.json()` / `res.end()`)
3. Log: method, path, user ID/email (from `req.user`), response status, elapsed time
4. Optionally log request body and response body if needed

**Implementation shape:**
```javascript
app.use(sessionUser);

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on('finish', () => {
    const elapsed = Date.now() - start;
    console.log(`${req.method} ${req.path} | user: ${req.user.id} | status: ${res.statusCode} | ${elapsed}ms`);
  });
  next();
});

app.use("/accounts", accounts);
app.use("/orders", orders);
```

This logs every request after the response completes, so you see actual status codes. Alternatively, use Morgan middleware if you want a mature logger with filtering, format templates, and stream support.

For database-level logging (which queries ran, how long they took), you could add similar logging to `db.query()` and `db.write()` to see the actual SQL times—useful for spotting slow queries.

