Found it. The rate limiter silently bypasses when Redis fails.

In **redis-client.js**, all Redis commands swallow errors and return `null` on failure (lines 18-23):
```javascript
async incr(key) {
  try {
    return await client.incr(key);
  } catch (err) {
    return null;
  }
}
```

Back in **limiter.js** line 12, when `redis.incr()` returns `null`:
```javascript
const count = await redis.incr(key);
if (count > MAX_REQUESTS) {  // null > 100 is false
  return res.status(429).json({ error: 'rate limit exceeded' });
}
```

Since `null > 100` evaluates to `false`, the rate limit check passes and the request goes through. Every request during a Redis outage bypasses the limit completely.

If Redis had a blip or timeout yesterday (the 200ms connectTimeout in redis-client.js line 5 is tight), all 12,000 requests would have gotten `null` from `incr()`, triggering no 429s.

